<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Witwatersrand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as the Rand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Witwatersrand is a low ridge and the surrounding region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, where the world's richest gold deposits were discovered in 1886. The gold rush created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and transformed the South African Republic, drawing foreign miners whose grievances helped bring on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>. Its deep mines drew migrant labour from across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/witwatersrand.jpg" alt="Witwatersrand" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Eason from London · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waterfall,_Witwatersrand_National_Botanical_Gardens.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Diamond mining at Kimberley pioneered the compound system and the large mining houses, notably De Beers, that later dominated the gold fields of the <b>Witwatersrand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Everything changed with the discovery of the Main Reef gold deposits on the <b>Witwatersrand</b> in 1886.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-african-republic">South African Republic · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union’s economy rested on mining, above all the gold of the <b>Witwatersrand</b>, which made South Africa the world’s largest gold producer, together with the diamonds of Kimberley, coal and commercial farming.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The company ruled through administrators such as Leander Starr Jameson and William Milton, granting land and mining claims to settlers in hope of a second <b>Witwatersrand</b> that never appeared.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>Later rulers profited from sending men to work on the Kimberley and <b>Witwatersrand</b> mines.</blockquote><a href="/state/gaza-empire">Gaza Empire</a></li></ul></section>
